const toc = [
  { id: 'objectives', label: 'Learning objectives' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits' },
  { id: 'continuity', label: 'Continuity and discontinuities' },
  { id: 'derivatives', label: 'Derivatives' },
  { id: 'shape', label: 'What f′ tells you about f' },
  { id: 'integrals', label: 'Integrals and Riemann sums' },
  { id: 'signed', label: 'Signed area' },
  { id: 'ftc', label: 'The fundamental theorem' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'Formula reference' },
  { id: 'numerical', label: 'How the lab computes' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'facts', label: 'Interesting facts' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Important terms' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Strang, G., & Herman, E. (2016). Calculus Volume 1, Chapter 2: Limits. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/2-introduction' },
  { text: 'Strang, G., & Herman, E. (2016). Calculus Volume 1, Chapter 3: Derivatives. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/3-introduction' },
  { text: 'Strang, G., & Herman, E. (2016). Calculus Volume 1, Sections 5.1–5.3: Approximating Areas, The Definite Integral, The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/5-introduction' },
  { text: 'Burden, R. L., Faires, J. D., & Burden, A. M. (2016). Numerical Analysis (10th ed.), Chapter 4: Numerical Differentiation and Integration. Cengage.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="mathematics"
  topic="calculus"
  description="Interactive calculus explorer: approach limits from both sides, drag tangent and secant lines, estimate areas with Riemann sums and see the fundamental theorem of calculus link derivatives and integrals."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={calculusQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Calculus is the mathematics of change. It answers two questions: how fast is something changing right now (the
      derivative), and how much has built up over time (the integral). Both rest on the idea of a limit. Approach a point
      from both sides, turn a secant into a tangent, pack thinner and thinner rectangles under a curve, and watch the two big
      ideas of calculus turn out to be opposite sides of one coin.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <CalculusSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>function</strong>, or type your own (for example <code>x^3 - 2x</code> or <code>sin(2x)/x</code>) and press <strong>Plot</strong>. Sliders a and b appear when the function uses them.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Limits</strong>, drag the target marker on the x-axis, then drag the purple (left) and orange (right) points towards it, or press <strong>Start</strong>. Compare the table of values and read the verdict.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Derivative</strong>, drag P along the curve and Q towards P. Turn on the graph of f′ and the increasing/decreasing bands to see where f rises, falls and turns.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Integral</strong>, drag the bounds a and b, change the number of rectangles and the rule, and press <strong>Start</strong> to watch the sum converge.</li>
      <li>In <strong>FTC</strong>, drag x and watch the accumulated area A(x) in the lower graph. Its slope always matches the height of f.</li>
      <li>Drag the background to pan, pinch or Ctrl + scroll to zoom, and tap the graph to read coordinates. Try the <strong>challenges</strong> under the controls.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="objectives">Learning objectives</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Estimate one-sided and two-sided limits, and explain why a limit can differ from a function’s value.</li>
    <li>Recognise continuous functions and removable, jump and infinite discontinuities.</li>
    <li>Interpret the derivative as the limit of secant slopes and the slope of the tangent line.</li>
    <li>Use the sign of f′ to find where f increases or decreases, and classify stationary points correctly.</li>
    <li>Approximate definite integrals with left, right and midpoint Riemann sums, and distinguish signed area from geometric area.</li>
    <li>Explain the fundamental theorem of calculus.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>limit</strong> of f(x) as x approaches a describes the values f(x) gets close to when x is close to a, but
    not equal to it. We write lim<sub>x→a</sub> f(x) = L. The value f(a) itself does not matter: f might not even be defined
    at a.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>left-hand limit</strong> (x → a⁻) uses only x &lt; a; a <strong>right-hand limit</strong> (x → a⁺) uses only
    x &gt; a. The two-sided limit exists only when both one-sided limits exist and are equal. If f grows without bound, we
    write lim = ∞ to describe the behaviour, but the limit does not exist as a number.
  </p>

  <h2 id="continuity">Continuity and discontinuities</h2>
  <p>f is <strong>continuous</strong> at a when three things hold: f(a) is defined, the limit exists, and the limit equals f(a).</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What happens</th><th>Example in the lab</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Removable (a hole)</td><td>The limit exists but f(a) is undefined or different</td><td>(x² − 1)/(x − 1) at x = 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jump</td><td>Left and right limits exist but are different</td><td>x² (x &lt; 1), x + 2 (x ≥ 1)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Infinite</td><td>f grows without bound: a vertical asymptote</td><td>1/(x − 2)² at 2, 1/x at 0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Oscillating</td><td>f keeps swinging and never settles</td><td>sin(1/x) at 0</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="derivatives">Derivatives</h2>
  <p>
    The slope of a <strong>secant line</strong> through (x, f(x)) and (x + h, f(x + h)) is the <strong>average rate of
    change</strong> [f(x + h) − f(x)]/h. Letting h shrink to zero gives the <strong>derivative</strong>, the
    <strong>instantaneous rate of change</strong>:
  </p>
  <p><strong>f′(x) = lim<sub>h→0</sub> [f(x + h) − f(x)] / h</strong></p>
  <p>
    Geometrically, f′(x) is the slope of the <strong>tangent line</strong>, the line that just touches the curve there. A
    function is <strong>differentiable</strong> at x when this limit exists. Corners (|x| at 0), cusps (x<sup>2/3</sup> at 0),
    jumps and vertical tangents have no derivative. A differentiable function is always continuous, but a continuous function
    need not be differentiable.
  </p>

  <h2 id="shape">What f′ tells you about f</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Where f′(x) &gt; 0, f is <strong>increasing</strong>; where f′(x) &lt; 0, f is <strong>decreasing</strong>.</li>
    <li>Where f′(x) = 0, the tangent is horizontal: a <strong>stationary point</strong>.</li>
    <li>A stationary point is a <strong>local maximum</strong> if f′ changes from + to −, a <strong>local minimum</strong> if it changes from − to +, and <strong>neither</strong> if f′ keeps its sign (x³ at 0).</li>
    <li>Maxima and minima can also occur where f′ does not exist, such as the corner of |x|.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="integrals">Integrals and Riemann sums</h2>
  <p>
    To find the area under a curve between x = a and x = b, split [a, b] into n strips of width Δx = (b − a)/n, and add up
    rectangles of height f(xᵢ*), where xᵢ* is the left end, right end or midpoint of each strip. This is a
    <strong>Riemann sum</strong>, Σ f(xᵢ*)·Δx. As n → ∞ the sums approach the <strong>definite integral</strong>
    ∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(x) dx.
  </p>
  <p>
    An <strong>indefinite integral</strong> ∫ f(x) dx = F(x) + C is the family of all <strong>antiderivatives</strong>
    (functions whose derivative is f). A definite integral is a number; an indefinite integral is a family of functions.
    When F is known, ∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a).
  </p>
  <p>
    For an increasing function, left sums underestimate and right sums overestimate; midpoint sums are usually much more
    accurate. The error of left and right sums shrinks roughly in proportion to 1/n, and of midpoint sums to 1/n².
  </p>

  <h2 id="signed">Signed area versus geometric area</h2>
  <p>
    The definite integral measures <strong>signed</strong> accumulation: area above the x-axis counts positive and area
    below counts negative. So ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>2π</sup> sin x dx = 0, even though the curve encloses a total
    <strong>geometric area</strong> of ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>2π</sup> |sin x| dx = 4. If f is unbounded on [a, b] (for example 1/x
    across 0), the ordinary integral does not exist; such “improper” integrals need separate treatment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ftc">The fundamental theorem of calculus</h2>
  <p>
    Define the accumulation function A(x) = ∫<sub>a</sub><sup>x</sup> f(t) dt: the signed area from a up to a moving edge x.
    If f is continuous on an interval containing a and x, then
  </p>
  <p><strong>d/dx [ ∫<sub>a</sub><sup>x</sup> f(t) dt ] = f(x)</strong></p>
  <p>
    In words: the area grows at a rate equal to the height of the curve at the moving edge. When f is positive A increases,
    when f is negative A decreases, and where f crosses zero A has a peak or a valley. The second part of the theorem gives
    ∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a) for any antiderivative F, which is how exact integrals are calculated.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formulas">Formula reference</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Formula</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>lim<sub>x→a</sub> f(x) = L</td><td>f(x) gets arbitrarily close to L as x gets close to a (x ≠ a)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>[f(x + h) − f(x)] / h</td><td>Secant slope: average rate of change over a step h</td></tr>
        <tr><td>f′(x) = lim<sub>h→0</sub> [f(x + h) − f(x)] / h</td><td>Derivative: instantaneous rate of change</td></tr>
        <tr><td>y = f(a) + f′(a)(x − a)</td><td>Tangent line at x = a</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Δx = (b − a)/n; Σ f(xᵢ*) Δx</td><td>Riemann sum with n strips</td></tr>
        <tr><td>∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a)</td><td>Definite integral from an antiderivative F (f continuous on [a, b])</td></tr>
        <tr><td>d/dx ∫<sub>a</sub><sup>x</sup> f(t) dt = f(x)</td><td>Fundamental theorem of calculus (f continuous)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>(xⁿ)′ = n·xⁿ⁻¹; (eᵏˣ)′ = k·eᵏˣ; (sin x)′ = cos x</td><td>Some derivatives used by the presets</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="numerical">How the lab computes</h2>
  <p>
    A computer cannot take a true limit, so the lab estimates. Limits are estimated from f(a ± 10⁻ᵏ) for k up to 7 and
    labelled “numerical”. Derivatives of the preset functions use their exact formulas; for your own functions the lab uses
    the central difference [f(x + h) − f(x − h)]/(2h) with a small h, which is accurate to about eight significant digits for smooth
    functions. Corners and cusps are detected by comparing the slopes from the left and the right. Exact integrals come from
    an antiderivative when one is known; otherwise the lab uses two-point Gauss–Legendre quadrature with 1,000 panels. The
    lab flags intervals where f is unbounded instead of printing a meaningless number. Expressions you type are read by a
    small parser that only understands maths, never by running code. Very rapidly oscillating or extremely steep functions
    can still fool any numerical method.
  </p>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Physics:</strong> velocity is the derivative of position, acceleration is the derivative of velocity, and distance travelled is the integral of speed.</li>
    <li><strong>Engineering:</strong> integrals give the work done by a force, the centre of mass of a beam and the charge stored in a capacitor.</li>
    <li><strong>Economics:</strong> marginal cost and marginal revenue are derivatives; total cost is the integral of marginal cost.</li>
    <li><strong>Medicine:</strong> the area under a drug-concentration curve measures total exposure to a medicine.</li>
    <li><strong>Computing:</strong> machine learning trains models by following derivatives (gradients) downhill.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="facts">Interesting facts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz developed calculus independently in the 1660s–1680s; the notation dy/dx and ∫ is Leibniz’s.</li>
    <li>Archimedes estimated the area of a parabolic segment more than 2,000 years ago by filling it with ever-smaller triangles, an early version of a limit.</li>
    <li>The rigorous ε–δ definition of a limit was only completed in the 19th century, by Cauchy and Weierstrass.</li>
    <li>Weierstrass found a function that is continuous everywhere but differentiable nowhere: a curve made entirely of “corners”.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“The limit is just f(a).”</strong> Only for continuous functions. A limit can exist where f(a) is undefined or different.</li>
    <li><strong>“f′(a) = 0 means a maximum or minimum.”</strong> Not always: x³ has f′(0) = 0 but no turning point.</li>
    <li><strong>“Continuous means differentiable.”</strong> |x| is continuous at 0 but has no derivative there.</li>
    <li><strong>“An integral is always the area.”</strong> It is signed area: parts below the axis subtract.</li>
    <li><strong>“The secant slope is the derivative.”</strong> The secant slope is an average rate of change; the derivative is its limit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="terms">Important terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Limit</dt>
    <dd>The value f(x) approaches as x approaches a, from one side or both.</dd>
    <dt>Continuity</dt>
    <dd>f is continuous at a if f(a) exists and equals the limit there.</dd>
    <dt>Derivative</dt>
    <dd>The instantaneous rate of change; the slope of the tangent line.</dd>
    <dt>Secant line</dt>
    <dd>A line through two points of a curve; its slope is an average rate of change.</dd>
    <dt>Tangent line</dt>
    <dd>The line through a point of a curve with slope equal to the derivative there.</dd>
    <dt>Differentiable</dt>
    <dd>Having a derivative at a point (no corner, cusp, jump or vertical tangent).</dd>
    <dt>Stationary point</dt>
    <dd>A point where f′ = 0.</dd>
    <dt>Riemann sum</dt>
    <dd>A sum of rectangle areas that approximates an integral.</dd>
    <dt>Definite integral</dt>
    <dd>The limit of Riemann sums on [a, b]: a number giving signed accumulation.</dd>
    <dt>Indefinite integral (antiderivative)</dt>
    <dd>A family of functions F(x) + C whose derivative is f.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A limit describes behaviour near a point, not the value at it.</li>
    <li>The derivative is the limit of secant slopes: the slope of the tangent.</li>
    <li>The sign of f′ shows where f rises and falls; turning points need f′ to change sign.</li>
    <li>Integrals are limits of Riemann sums and measure signed area.</li>
    <li>The fundamental theorem links the two ideas: accumulating f and then differentiating gives f back.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
